1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,570
A customer reports a bug that you fixed
last week. Your CTO asks one calm question:

2
00:00:09,610 --> 00:00:11,540
"Is the fix in what we shipped?"

3
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,973
You could open the release notes. You
could search the log for the message.

4
00:00:16,013 --> 00:00:20,381
Both can mislead you, because a message
can appear on a commit that isn't in the

5
00:00:20,421 --> 00:00:22,751
release. So how would
you know for certain?

6
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:29,735
The question is an ancestry question, and
Git answers it with an exit status, the

7
00:00:29,775 --> 00:00:34,305
number a command reports when it finishes:
is the fix commit reachable from the

8
00:00:34,345 --> 00:00:39,105
released commit, yes or no. To ask it, you
need to know what "reachable" means, and

9
00:00:39,145 --> 00:00:42,229
what a name such as main
really is. That's this video.

10
00:00:44,069 --> 00:00:47,757
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:47,797 --> 00:00:52,891
You now know the objects: blobs for file
content, trees for directories, commits for

12
00:00:52,931 --> 00:00:57,719
snapshots. This video is about how commits
connect to each other, and how names

13
00:00:57,759 --> 00:00:58,881
connect to commits.

14
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:04,117
The first half is the commit graph: parent
pointers, what "the history of X" means,

15
00:01:04,157 --> 00:01:07,151
and what happens to a commit
that no name leads to.

16
00:01:07,191 --> 00:01:12,088
The second half is refs and HEAD: you'll
read a branch as a file with one ID in it,

17
00:01:12,128 --> 00:01:16,370
watch a commit move that file, and
see the two forms that HEAD can take.

18
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:21,009
We close with a trap, a ref written under
the wrong name, because it shows how

19
00:01:21,049 --> 00:01:25,470
literally Git treats names. And along
the way, one command answers the CTO.

20
00:01:27,310 --> 00:01:28,889
Here's what you'll walk away with.

21
00:01:29,229 --> 00:01:30,641
After this video you can:

22
00:01:30,981 --> 00:01:34,441
Draw the commit graph of a small
history from its parent pointers.

23
00:01:34,781 --> 00:01:38,553
Define reachable, and decide for a
given commit whether a ref reaches it.

24
00:01:38,893 --> 00:01:43,694
Show that a branch is a file holding one
commit ID, and that HEAD names a branch.

25
00:01:44,034 --> 00:01:47,108
Rescue a commit that no ref
reaches by giving it a name.

26
00:01:48,948 --> 00:01:53,670
The commit graph. In one sentence: every
commit names its parent commits, so the

27
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:58,433
commits form a graph that is walked from
child to parent, and "the history of X"

28
00:01:58,473 --> 00:02:02,183
means everything you can reach
from X by following those links.

29
00:02:02,523 --> 00:02:06,496
Now precisely. The commits form
a directed acyclic graph, a DAG.

30
00:02:06,536 --> 00:02:11,450
It's directed because the link is stored
in the child and points at the parent.

31
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:16,655
It's acyclic because a commit's ID is
computed from its parents' IDs: a parent must

32
00:02:16,695 --> 00:02:20,982
exist before its child, so no chain
of links can return to its start.

33
00:02:21,322 --> 00:02:26,055
An object is reachable from another if a
chain leads to it that follows tags to what

34
00:02:26,095 --> 00:02:30,204
they tag, commits to their parents and
trees, and trees to their entries.

35
00:02:30,244 --> 00:02:34,920
If that felt fast, here it is in one sentence:
reachable means you can get there by

36
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:35,830
following links.

37
00:02:36,170 --> 00:02:39,086
Three things follow, and each
corrects a common belief.

38
00:02:39,426 --> 00:02:43,696
One: a commit doesn't know its children.
So "what came after this commit?"

39
00:02:43,736 --> 00:02:48,530
has an answer only relative to a starting
point. History commands start from a ref,

40
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:52,141
a name that points at a commit,
like the labels now on screen.

41
00:02:52,481 --> 00:02:55,395
Two: "the commits on a
branch" is not a stored list.

42
00:02:55,435 --> 00:02:58,861
It's the set reachable from the
commit that the branch names.

43
00:02:59,201 --> 00:03:03,278
Three: "A is an ancestor of B"
means that A is reachable from B.

44
00:03:03,318 --> 00:03:07,267
git merge-base --is-ancestor A
B answers with its exit status.

45
00:03:07,607 --> 00:03:11,942
And inside the dot git folder, the
graph isn't stored anywhere as a whole.

46
00:03:11,982 --> 00:03:16,139
Parent IDs are inside commit objects,
and the starting points are refs.

47
00:03:16,479 --> 00:03:21,712
Unreachable is not gone. A commit that no
ref reaches still exists as an object.

48
00:03:21,752 --> 00:03:27,183
Deleting a branch deletes a name, never a
commit. git gc, Git's garbage collection,

49
00:03:27,223 --> 00:03:32,193
removes unreachable objects only after
a grace period, two weeks by default.

50
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:37,202
The setting is gc.pruneExpire. Reflog
entries also count as starting points.

51
00:03:37,242 --> 00:03:40,959
A reflog is the local journal
of where a ref has pointed.

52
00:03:41,299 --> 00:03:47,123
Its entries are kept for 90 days by default,
or 30 days once the commit is no longer

53
00:03:47,163 --> 00:03:52,080
reachable from the tip of its ref.
The settings are gc.reflogExpire and

54
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:57,735
gc.reflogExpireUnreachable. The lab
configuration sets both of those to never, as

55
00:03:57,775 --> 00:04:02,343
video 1 explained. The recovery
chapter is built on these numbers.

56
00:04:02,683 --> 00:04:07,666
Refs and HEAD. In one sentence: a ref is a
name for an object ID, a branch is a ref

57
00:04:07,706 --> 00:04:12,690
that moves forward when you commit, and
HEAD records which branch, or which commit,

58
00:04:12,730 --> 00:04:13,356
you are on.

59
00:04:13,696 --> 00:04:19,079
Now precisely. A ref refers either to an
object ID or to another ref, in which case

60
00:04:19,119 --> 00:04:23,915
it's a symbolic ref. Its place in the
hierarchy decides how Git treats it.

61
00:04:24,255 --> 00:04:29,575
refs/heads/<name> is a branch. It refers
to a commit, and it moves when you commit,

62
00:04:29,615 --> 00:04:33,062
merge, reset or rebase while
it is the current branch.

63
00:04:33,402 --> 00:04:38,504
refs/tags/<name> is a tag. It refers
usually to a commit or a tag object, and it

64
00:04:38,544 --> 00:04:39,854
normally never moves.

65
00:04:40,194 --> 00:04:43,444
refs/remotes/<remote>/<name>
is a remote-tracking branch.

66
00:04:43,484 --> 00:04:48,177
It refers to a commit, and it moves when
you fetch from that remote or push to it.

67
00:04:48,517 --> 00:04:53,426
HEAD refers to the current branch, as a
symbolic ref, or to one commit ID directly,

68
00:04:53,466 --> 00:04:56,765
which is called detached HEAD.
It moves when you switch.

69
00:04:57,105 --> 00:05:01,902
A branch contains no commits. It names
one, and its history is what is reachable

70
00:05:01,942 --> 00:05:05,771
from there. Creating a branch
writes one ref and copies nothing.

71
00:05:05,811 --> 00:05:10,849
And Git records no "parent branch", so merge
and rebase must be told the other side.

72
00:05:11,189 --> 00:05:15,540
A caution about files. In this video
each ref is a file that cat can read.

73
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:20,228
That's the default "files" format, not a
guarantee: refs can be packed into one

74
00:05:20,268 --> 00:05:23,313
file, and the reftable format
stores them in binary.

75
00:05:23,353 --> 00:05:27,823
git rev-parse, git symbolic-ref and git
for-each-ref work with every format.

76
00:05:28,163 --> 00:05:31,666
Risk labels. git branch <name>
is 🟢 SAFE: it adds one ref.

77
00:05:31,706 --> 00:05:36,674
git switch is 🟢 SAFE: it changes HEAD, the
index and the working tree, and refuses

78
00:05:36,714 --> 00:05:41,622
to overwrite uncommitted changes. git
update-ref is 🟡 CAUTION: it changes one ref

79
00:05:41,662 --> 00:05:42,660
and nothing else.

80
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:49,436
For the graph, the textbook's analogy is
a family tree in which each record lists

81
00:05:49,476 --> 00:05:54,596
the parents and none lists the children.
From any person you can find all ancestors,

82
00:05:54,636 --> 00:05:58,282
but to find descendants you must
start from someone younger.

83
00:05:58,322 --> 00:06:03,073
It breaks on the counts: a commit has no
parent, which makes it a root commit.

84
00:06:03,113 --> 00:06:06,452
Or one parent, an ordinary
commit. Or several, a merge.

85
00:06:06,792 --> 00:06:10,467
For refs: sticky labels on a
wall chart of the commit graph.

86
00:06:10,507 --> 00:06:15,420
Moving a label changes nothing on the
chart, and removing one removes no commit.

87
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:20,559
HEAD is the "you are here" marker, normally
stuck onto a label rather than onto the

88
00:06:20,599 --> 00:06:25,636
chart. It breaks because labels are local:
every clone has its own set, and learns

89
00:06:25,676 --> 00:06:29,042
about another repository's
labels only when it fetches.

90
00:06:29,382 --> 00:06:32,955
Combine them. The chart is
permanent and shared by content.

91
00:06:32,995 --> 00:06:38,100
The labels are small, movable and yours.
Almost every Git command you'll learn moves

92
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:38,529
labels.

93
00:06:40,369 --> 00:06:46,503
Time flows left to right. Two commits, then
a fork: one commit on one line, one on

94
00:06:46,543 --> 00:06:51,472
the other. They join in 2511274,
the merge, which has two parents.

95
00:06:51,512 --> 00:06:56,969
main points there, and HEAD names main.
feature/dedupe points at ea0d3fa.

96
00:06:57,009 --> 00:07:03,068
The last commit on the right, 2c65cfc, is
the one we'll create without a name and

97
00:07:03,108 --> 00:07:03,971
then rescue.

98
00:07:04,311 --> 00:07:09,270
Before: two labels on one commit, and HEAD
attached to the label feature/unicode.

99
00:07:09,310 --> 00:07:14,392
After: a new commit whose parent is the old
one. The label feature/unicode moved to

100
00:07:14,432 --> 00:07:19,205
it. main didn't move. HEAD didn't move
either: it's still attached to the same

101
00:07:19,245 --> 00:07:21,180
label, and the label carried it.

102
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:24,917
Five commits, one of them a merge.

103
00:07:25,257 --> 00:07:30,351
Try it now. Before the next snippet, draw
this graph on paper from the picture, with

104
00:07:30,391 --> 00:07:35,178
arrows from child to parent. Thirty
seconds. Then check your answer against the

105
00:07:35,218 --> 00:07:36,156
parent pointers.

106
00:07:40,996 --> 00:07:46,476
%p prints the parents. The merge commit
2511274 has two parent lines: first the

107
00:07:46,516 --> 00:07:52,136
commit that main pointed at when the merge
was made, 6d7e946, then the tip of the

108
00:07:52,176 --> 00:07:57,656
merged branch, ea0d3fa. A merge commit
is an ordinary commit with more than one

109
00:07:57,696 --> 00:08:02,756
parent, and its tree is the merged
snapshot. The root, dec99b5, has none.

110
00:08:02,796 --> 00:08:08,416
Did your arrows point from old to new?
Almost everyone draws them that way first.

111
00:08:08,456 --> 00:08:13,237
Git stores them the other way: each
child points back at its parents.

112
00:08:13,577 --> 00:08:18,957
Predict: how many commits are reachable from
main, and how many from feature/dedupe?

113
00:08:18,997 --> 00:08:20,699
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

114
00:08:25,539 --> 00:08:30,689
Five from main. Three from feature/dedupe:
its own commit and the two it shares with

115
00:08:30,729 --> 00:08:35,199
main. And the ancestry test: the branch
is an ancestor of main, status 0.

116
00:08:35,239 --> 00:08:40,327
main is not an ancestor of the branch,
status 1. That's the command for "is the fix

117
00:08:40,367 --> 00:08:43,910
in what we shipped?". One line,
and the CTO has an answer.

118
00:08:44,250 --> 00:08:48,961
Now a commit with no name. git commit-tree
writes a commit object and moves no ref.

119
00:08:49,301 --> 00:08:55,770
Stop here. A sixth commit was created,
2c65cfc, whose parent is the tip of main.

120
00:08:55,810 --> 00:08:57,071
Where is it now?

121
00:08:59,411 --> 00:09:03,718
It's in the object database:
git cat-file -t says commit.

122
00:09:03,758 --> 00:09:09,894
But git rev-list --count --all still counts
five, and git fsck reports a dangling

123
00:09:09,934 --> 00:09:13,097
commit. It exists, and no ref leads to it.

124
00:09:13,437 --> 00:09:16,794
git branch rescue <id> 🟢
SAFE created a ref for it.

125
00:09:16,834 --> 00:09:21,990
Six commits are reachable, git fsck is
silent, and the graph shows the commit.

126
00:09:22,030 --> 00:09:24,388
No object changed. A name was added.

127
00:09:24,728 --> 00:09:27,539
One commit on main. We read the ref files.

128
00:09:27,879 --> 00:09:32,341
HEAD contains the name of a branch, and
the branch file contains a commit ID.

129
00:09:32,381 --> 00:09:36,901
git rev-parse resolves both names to that
ID. git symbolic-ref HEAD prints the

130
00:09:36,941 --> 00:09:38,246
branch that HEAD names.

131
00:09:38,586 --> 00:09:43,351
Quick quiz, three options. git branch
feature/unicode creates a branch.

132
00:09:43,391 --> 00:09:47,681
How many objects does it add?
Option one: none. Option two: one.

133
00:09:47,721 --> 00:09:51,335
Option three: a copy of every
commit. Say it out loud.

134
00:09:56,175 --> 00:10:00,072
Option one: none. The object list
is identical before and after.

135
00:10:00,112 --> 00:10:05,054
The command wrote one small file holding
one ID. git for-each-ref lists every ref

136
00:10:05,094 --> 00:10:07,945
with the object it names:
two refs, one commit.

137
00:10:08,285 --> 00:10:12,722
Now we switch and commit. Predict
which of three files change: HEAD,

138
00:10:12,762 --> 00:10:15,619
refs/heads/main,
refs/heads/feature/unicode.

139
00:10:20,459 --> 00:10:24,257
git switch 🟢 SAFE rewrote HEAD
to name the other branch.

140
00:10:24,297 --> 00:10:29,877
The commit then moved feature/unicode to
4a3fb80. main stayed at 3046dc6, and HEAD

141
00:10:29,917 --> 00:10:32,824
reads the same before
and after the commit.

142
00:10:33,164 --> 00:10:37,595
Here's that as a picture. A commit moves
the current branch, and HEAD follows

143
00:10:37,635 --> 00:10:39,278
because it names that branch.

144
00:10:39,618 --> 00:10:43,282
Older scripts switch branches
with git checkout. Same effect.

145
00:10:43,322 --> 00:10:46,683
You see it once here so that
you can read those scripts.

146
00:10:47,023 --> 00:10:52,066
The second form of HEAD. git switch
--detach wrote a commit ID into HEAD.

147
00:10:52,106 --> 00:10:56,383
git symbolic-ref fails, because
HEAD isn't a symbolic ref now.

148
00:10:56,423 --> 00:11:00,491
No branch is current, so a new
commit would move no branch.

149
00:11:00,531 --> 00:11:05,922
Switching back to main restores the
symbolic form. Video 24 is devoted to this

150
00:11:05,962 --> 00:11:06,340
state.

151
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:10,775
The trap. The intent is to move the
branch main to the first commit.

152
00:11:10,815 --> 00:11:15,519
The name given to git update-ref 🟡 CAUTION
is main instead of refs/heads/main.

153
00:11:15,559 --> 00:11:16,674
Predict what moves.

154
00:11:17,014 --> 00:11:22,400
The command printed nothing, and the branch
did not move: git branch -vv still shows

155
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:27,697
cfa6dbc. But the name main now resolves to
the old commit, with a warning that the

156
00:11:27,737 --> 00:11:28,860
name is ambiguous.

157
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:33,498
git for-each-ref, which lists
refs/, doesn't show the stray ref.

158
00:11:33,538 --> 00:11:38,378
A listing of the top of .git does: a
file .git/main, holding the old ID.

159
00:11:38,418 --> 00:11:43,122
git update-ref -d main is refused,
and the fix is to delete that file.

160
00:11:43,462 --> 00:11:48,453
The mechanism: update-ref writes exactly
the ref it is given, and for an update it

161
00:11:48,493 --> 00:11:53,484
checks only that the name is well-formed.
Name lookup tries the name at the top of

162
00:11:53,524 --> 00:11:58,331
.git before refs/heads/. For a deletion,
Git accepts only names under refs/ and

163
00:11:58,371 --> 00:12:00,539
names in capitals such as ORIG_HEAD.

164
00:12:00,879 --> 00:12:05,948
The root cause: a plumbing command received
a short name where it needs the full ref

165
00:12:05,988 --> 00:12:11,057
name. Why Git does this: plumbing serves
scripts. It does what it's told and guesses

166
00:12:11,097 --> 00:12:16,105
nothing. Prevention: write refs/heads/<name>
for update-ref, and move branches with

167
00:12:16,145 --> 00:12:18,112
porcelain, the everyday commands.

168
00:12:18,452 --> 00:12:23,902
The textbook notes that the two name checks
were read from the Git 2.55.0 source,

169
00:12:23,942 --> 00:12:28,511
and that the fix applies to the
files format, where a ref is a file.

170
00:12:30,351 --> 00:12:31,999
Five mistakes to watch for.

171
00:12:32,339 --> 00:12:36,962
Thinking a branch contains commits. Root
cause: a branch is a ref that names one

172
00:12:37,002 --> 00:12:40,400
commit; "its commits" are
whatever is reachable from there.

173
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:45,471
Believing that deleting a branch deletes
commits. Root cause: deleting a branch

174
00:12:45,511 --> 00:12:50,423
deletes a name; unreachable objects remain
until garbage collection, after a grace

175
00:12:50,463 --> 00:12:50,846
period.

176
00:12:51,186 --> 00:12:55,965
Reading HEAD as "the latest commit". Root
cause: HEAD is a symbolic ref to the

177
00:12:56,005 --> 00:13:00,722
current branch, or a direct reference
to a commit, in which case no branch is

178
00:13:00,762 --> 00:13:01,217
current.

179
00:13:01,557 --> 00:13:06,734
A name such as main is reported ambiguous.
Root cause: a stray ref outside refs/,

180
00:13:06,774 --> 00:13:09,568
written by git update-ref
with a short name.

181
00:13:09,908 --> 00:13:12,977
Answering "which commit is
deployed?" with a branch name.

182
00:13:13,017 --> 00:13:17,503
Root cause: main is a moving name; it meant
another commit yesterday and may mean a

183
00:13:17,543 --> 00:13:19,848
different one in a
colleague's clone today.

184
00:13:21,688 --> 00:13:26,454
Now, out of the lab. A backend team ships
release builds from tagged commits.

185
00:13:26,494 --> 00:13:31,572
A fix for a retry bug is merged, and a
customer on the previous release still sees

186
00:13:31,612 --> 00:13:36,066
the bug. "Is the fix in what we
shipped?" is settled with git merge-base

187
00:13:36,106 --> 00:13:41,309
--is-ancestor <fix> <released commit>: exit
status 1 means the released commit can't

188
00:13:41,349 --> 00:13:44,617
reach the fix. Nobody reads
a changelog to decide it.

189
00:13:44,957 --> 00:13:49,354
And the textbook's rule about names in
production: "Which commit is deployed?"

190
00:13:49,394 --> 00:13:53,847
must be answered with a commit ID. A tag
is a name that isn't supposed to move.

191
00:13:53,887 --> 00:13:58,511
Whether it can is decided by the rules on
the server, a GitHub matter that a later

192
00:13:58,551 --> 00:13:59,364
chapter covers.

193
00:14:01,204 --> 00:14:07,653
Your turn. Do Exercise 1.6, Level
2, "two branches and a tag", in

194
00:14:07,693 --> 00:14:10,948
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

195
00:14:11,288 --> 00:14:16,851
Draw the graph on paper first, with
refs and HEAD, from the commands alone.

196
00:14:16,891 --> 00:14:22,903
Only then run git log --graph --decorate
--oneline --all and compare your drawing

197
00:14:22,943 --> 00:14:23,501
with it.

198
00:14:25,341 --> 00:14:30,032
Q19: "Define "reachable". Why is a commit
that no ref can reach still in the

199
00:14:30,072 --> 00:14:32,585
repository, and for how long by default?"

200
00:14:34,925 --> 00:14:39,791
Answer out loud. A strong answer gives the
definition as a chain of links and says

201
00:14:39,831 --> 00:14:44,698
which links count. It separates the existence
of an object from the existence of a

202
00:14:44,738 --> 00:14:49,425
name for it. For "how long", it names the
mechanisms that keep or remove such a

203
00:14:49,465 --> 00:14:54,272
commit and the default periods the manual
gives, and it knows that reflog entries

204
00:14:54,312 --> 00:14:55,709
are starting points too.

205
00:14:57,549 --> 00:15:02,333
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: each commit stores the IDs of its

206
00:15:02,373 --> 00:15:05,847
parents, so history is a graph
walked from child to parent.

207
00:15:05,887 --> 00:15:10,672
Reachable means there is a chain of such
links from a starting point, and history

208
00:15:10,712 --> 00:15:15,675
commands start from refs. A commit that no
ref reaches still exists, and giving it a

209
00:15:15,715 --> 00:15:17,462
name makes it reachable again.

210
00:15:17,802 --> 00:15:22,705
A branch is a ref holding one commit ID.
HEAD normally names a branch, and a commit

211
00:15:22,745 --> 00:15:27,171
moves that branch while HEAD stays
attached to it. And plumbing takes names

212
00:15:27,211 --> 00:15:29,672
literally, so I write refs/heads/ in full.

213
00:15:31,512 --> 00:15:34,813
Read sections 2.7 and 2.8 of Chapter 2.

214
00:15:35,153 --> 00:15:40,345
Draw the graph of Lab 1.1, "Build a commit
by hand", and of the guided repository on

215
00:15:40,385 --> 00:15:41,964
paper, with refs and HEAD.

216
00:15:42,304 --> 00:15:49,113
Challenge: Exercise 1.9, Level 4, "git
log says there are no commits", in

217
00:15:49,153 --> 00:15:52,209
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

218
00:15:52,549 --> 00:15:57,195
Today a branch stopped being a mystery:
it's one small file with one ID in it.

219
00:15:57,235 --> 00:16:02,241
If reachability still feels slippery, that's
normal, and drawing the homework graphs

220
00:16:02,281 --> 00:16:07,167
on paper is the cure. Next time: three
trees, two views of a commit, and the wrong

221
00:16:07,207 --> 00:16:11,193
mental models. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

222
00:16:11,233 --> 00:16:12,634
See you in the next one.
